For fixed \(M\), the condition \(N\in \mathcal K_{\mathcal F}^{(M)}\) depends only on a finite prefix of the reversed Zeckendorf expansion, so its characteristic sequence is of the morphic/automatic type associated with Fibonacci numeration.

A standard consequence for subsequences sampled on geometric progressions \(u q^n\) is:

- \(S_u^{(M)}\) is effectively **either finite or ultimately periodic**;
- one can **decide** which case occurs;
- one can **compute** an eventual preperiod \(n_0\) and period \(p\);
- and in the finite case, one can **output all elements** of \(S_u^{(M)}\).

The stronger claims of a universal pair independent of \(u\), or the explicit bound \(3^M\), are not the general statement.

